# veh-cluster corpus notes

The ridl reference **Appendix A**, compiled. `cluster/appendix-a.ridl` is the
appendix text verbatim — no line is adapted for the corpus — so every
diagnostic it draws is a fact about the reference, recorded below rather than
edited away. `common/` supplies the `veh.common` vocabulary Appendix A imports;
it is the typl reference Appendix B example, the same text the `veh-common`
entry compiles standalone.

`cluster/services.ridl` is authored on top: Appendix A declares no `service`,
and §14.5 needs both service forms exercised.

## What the entry covers

- **Both service forms.** Two named-reference services over one interface shape
  (`veh.cluster.status` and `veh.cluster.standby` both realize `VehicleStatus`)
  and one inline shape (`veh.hvac.cabin`).
- **The inline shape carries the whole surface.** A service's inline shape is
  an `Interface` stored under `Service.shape`, outside `Package.interfaces`, so
  a consumer that walks `package.interfaces` alone skips it silently — the
  shape of five separate E2 defects. `veh.hvac.cabin` therefore holds all five
  interaction kinds, every timing mode, contracts including one that reads
  `result`, an inline `T | E` fallible return, streams in both positions, a
  `reserved` tombstone, a deprecated member, and both `final` forms. The IR,
  Rust and TypeScript snapshots record what each consumer made of them.
- **Every timing mode.** Strict periodic (`@1s`, `@500us`), a range with both
  bounds, lower-bound-only, upper-bound-only, and one untimed interaction whose
  bounds come from the package `[defaults].timing`. The IR carries resolved
  microsecond bounds in every case, with `default_applied` set only on the last.
- **Ordinals with a gap, in both tombstone forms.** `reserved setEcoMode` holds
  ordinal 8 in the inline shape and `reserved resetCounters` holds ordinal 6 in
  `VehicleStatus`. `cluster/evolution.ridl` adds the *nameless* form,
  `reserved <ordinal>`, in a named interface and in an inline shape — the two
  stores lower through separate loops. The load-bearing consumer is
  `tools/diff/src/classify.rs::slots`, which folds tombstones into the ordinal
  sequence so a freed slot never looks free; without that a new interaction
  reusing a retired wire identity classifies as a clean append.
- **The whole guaranteed expression subset.** Comparison, boolean connectives,
  enum access, tuple-field access and duration comparison come from Appendix A
  and the inline shape; conjunction, arithmetic over a parameter, arithmetic
  over `result`, and a reference to a declared `const` (`MAX_FAN`) are the three
  members appended at the end of `veh.hvac.cabin`. They are appended rather than
  inserted so the ordinals above — and the synthesized transport identity
  `veh.hvac.cabin#13` — do not move.
- **Cross-package references.** Appendix A's five imports resolve through the
  workspace member, and `ridl.std` types (`Label`, `Message`, `Timestamp`,
  `Version`) resolve without an import.
- **`internal` on the interaction layer.** `cluster/internal-shape.ridl`
  declares `internal interface WheelDiagnostics` beside a public
  `interface WheelSummary`, so the generated output shows both side by side.
  This is the file that caught the visibility defect described under
  "`internal` was dropped by both backends" below; the snapshots now pin the
  repaired output. `WheelDiagnostics` also names `internal type RawTickCount`
  as a payload, a parameter and a return, which makes it the **legal** case of
  the exposure rule issue #161 closed: an `internal` interface over an
  `internal` payload must keep compiling, and both sides must generate
  `pub(crate)`. `rustc_accepts` denies `private_interfaces` by name, so that is
  a compile proof rather than a snapshot comparison — with the payload public,
  as it was before, the same proof passes even with the #160 defect back in
  place.
- **The generated code compiles, in both backends.**
  `veh_cluster_generated_rust_compiles_with_rustc` composes the two packages as
  sibling modules and runs `rustc` over the result;
  `veh_cluster_generated_typescript_type_checks` runs the emitted modules
  through `tsc --noEmit --strict` against the `tests/tsc/ridl.std.ts` stand-in.
  Both snapshots are therefore proofs rather than transcripts, which is what the
  E2 exit criterion — IR neutrality demonstrated by two backends — actually
  asks for. The tsc proof skips with a printed notice when no tsc is
  discoverable (the convention `backends/typescript` set), so Node stays
  optional for `cargo test`; the generated-source markers are asserted either
  way, so it never passes by type-checking nothing.

  _Known limitation._ Under a plain `cargo test` libtest captures the skip
  notice, so a skipped run reports a bare `ok` and is indistinguishable from a
  checked one without `--nocapture`. This matches the `backends/typescript`
  precedent and the markers keep the test non-vacuous either way. If it is
  closed later, the shape is an opt-in `RIDL_REQUIRE_TSC=1` that turns the skip
  into a failure.

## Diagnostics the entry draws, and why they are kept

The entry has no errors, so it is a full-pipeline golden. It carries two
warnings, both of them true statements about the source:

- **TYPL-007, unused import `MAX_SPEED`** — Appendix A imports
  `veh.common.MAX_SPEED` and never uses it. A fact about the appendix text.
- **RIDL-100, untimed event** — deliberate, in `services.ridl`: it is the only
  way to exercise the default-applied path end to end.

**RIDL-308 no longer fires here, and that is the recorded change.** Appendix A
wrote `query getFaultPage(filter: DiagFilter): FaultPageResult` over a named
`union FaultPageResult`, which drew the lint. General form §6.1 made the inline
`FaultPage | DiagError` spelling canonical in return position after the appendix
was written, and the E2 close-out adopted it in the reference text (ADR-0008
decisions 1 and 19); the appendix now writes the inline form and the union
declaration is deleted with it, because adopting the inline return leaves the
union unreferenced and no pass reports an unused declaration — TYPL-007 covers
unused imports only. RIDL-308 keeps a living example in `ridl-diag-showcase`,
which provokes it independently.

## Findings this entry pins

Nothing here is worked around. Each is written the way the toolchain behaves, so
the snapshots pin the behaviour and a repair shows up as a snapshot change. One
of them — `internal` on the interaction layer — has since been repaired that
way; its history is kept below rather than deleted, because what the corpus
found and why the coverage exists is part of what the corpus is for.

### `internal` was dropped by both backends — found here, fixed in #160

**Status: fixed.** Recorded because this entry is what found it.

`internal` is accepted on an `interface` (on an interaction or a service it is a
parse error), the checker honours it, and the IR records it faithfully:
`WheelDiagnostics` lowers with `visibility: 2` where `WheelSummary` and
`VehicleStatus` carry `1`. `ridl diff` models it too. Between PRs #155/#156
and #160, both backends then threw it away:

- **Rust** emitted `pub trait WheelDiagnosticsConsumer`,
  `pub trait WheelDiagnosticsProvider`, `pub const WHEEL_DIAGNOSTICS_TIMING` and
  `pub const WHEEL_DIAGNOSTICS_CONTRACTS` — byte-for-byte the same visibility as
  the public `WheelSummary`.
- **TypeScript** emitted `export interface WheelDiagnosticsConsumer`,
  `export interface WheelDiagnosticsProvider`, `export const
  wheelDiagnosticsTiming` and `export const wheelDiagnosticsContracts`.

A package-private contract shape leaked into both generated public surfaces — a
violation of **ADR-0002 §8** and **ADR-0008 decision 7**, not a gap in the
design. The control was in the same snapshots and is what bounded the
diagnosis: `internal struct RawWheelFrame` (typl, in `common/`) was already
correct in both backends, so the fault was the interaction layer specifically
and not `internal` in general.

**How it was found, and why that matters here.** The defect shipped in two
merged PRs and survived review of both backends. Nothing caught it until an
`internal interface` was added to this corpus — which is the argument for the
corpus in one sentence: a construct that no unit test exercised, compiled
through the real driver, beside its public counterpart, in output a human reads.

**The repair (#160).** Both backends now derive the visibility once per
interface from `Interface.visibility` and apply it to all four generated names,
so the two backends agree name for name. Deliberately unaffected, and asserted
as such: the interaction vocabulary (emitted once per module, shared by every
interface in it); the service table, since a service takes no `internal`
modifier (§14.5) and an inline shape's `VISIBILITY_UNSPECIFIED` maps to public;
and the Rust tuple structs an interaction position induces, which follow the
typl rule for a tuple under an `internal` declaration — TypeScript has no
counterpart, since a tuple is an inline object type there.

`internal_on_an_interface_is_package_private_in_both_backends` is now the
regression guard. It asserts all four names per backend in their fixed
spellings **and** the absence of all four leaked spellings, with the
`internal struct` control beside them. The four-per-backend shape is
deliberate: the first report of the defect named only the two faces and the
timing constant, and the contracts constant was found by enumerating what each
backend generates rather than by reading the report.

Two residues #160 could not reach, since they were checker behaviour rather than
codegen — issue **#161, now closed**. TYPL-005 ran over typl declarations only,
so a public `service` could publish an `internal` interface and a public
`interface` could carry an `internal` payload type, both undiagnosed. The second
was the sharper one: it made the Rust backend emit a `pub` trait over a
`pub(crate)` type, which draws rustc's `private_interfaces` lint and becomes a
hard build failure for any consumer building with `-D warnings`.

The repair moved the rule out of the typl definition lowering and into a
whole-file pass over the top-level items, so no declaration kind can escape it
by being added later; the service half took its own code, RIDL-143, because a
service cannot be made `internal` and so TYPL-005's second remedy does not
exist for it. The refusals are provoked in `ridl-diag-showcase`; the legal case
is here, in `cluster/internal-shape.ridl`.

### An inline shape carries no `Interface.visibility`

`Service.shape.Inline` lowers with `visibility: 0` (`VISIBILITY_UNSPECIFIED`)
where every named interface carries `1` (public). The reading recorded here, now
that the IR snapshot is golden: an inline shape has no name and is not
addressable as a type, so it has no visibility of its own — the enclosing
`Service` carries the authoritative one (`1` here), and a service cannot be
`internal` at all. A consumer must therefore read `Service.visibility`, never
`Service.shape.Inline.visibility`. If a consumer ever needs the field, the
inline shape should mirror its service rather than stay unspecified.
`ridl_ir::v2::InterfaceShape::visibility` is where that rule now lives, so a
consumer reading it through `Package::shapes` cannot get the unspecified value
by accident.

### The sixth inline-shape instance: RIDL-407 rendered without a span — fixed

Found by this corpus and pinned in `crates/ridl/tests/baseline_desk.rs`:
`DeclIndex::build` in `crates/ridl/src/main.rs` recorded inline-shape
*members*, but built the shape-level fallback — used when the named interaction
no longer exists in source — from `source.interfaces()` only. An interaction
removed from an inline shape therefore produced a RIDL-407 warning with no
file, no line and no snippet, where the same removal from a named interface
fell back to the interface name.

It was the sixth and last instance of one root cause: a consumer walking
`package.interfaces` (or `source.interfaces()`) as if it were the complete set
of interface bodies, when a service's inline shape lives outside it. All six
are closed. The structural repair is a single walk on each side —
`ridl_ir::v2::Package::shapes` and `ridl_syntax::ast::SourceFile::shapes` —
which yields a named interface and a service's inline shape alike, carrying the
identity name (the interface's own, or the service's dotted one) so no consumer
reads the inline shape's empty `Interface.name`, and the owning `Service` so no
consumer reads its unspecified `Interface.visibility` either (see the section
above). `DeclIndex` now walks `SourceFile::shapes()` and the inline-shape
warning spans the service's dotted name;
`inline_shape_removal_spans_the_service_name` pins that.

### A non-integer `reserved` literal is accepted and discarded

typl Appendix E defines `reserved = "reserved" ( camelCase_id | int_lit )`, but
the parser accepts **any** literal in that position (`Parser::reserved_entry`
falls through to `self.literal()`) and the lowering keeps only integers. So
`reserved "oldName"`, `reserved true` and `reserved 1.5` compile with **no
diagnostic at all** and lower to `Reserved { name: None, value: None }`.

The ordinal slot is still held, so the identity-reuse guarantee survives — but
nothing records *what* was retired, which is what `ridl diff` needs in order to
report a dangling tombstone (TYPL-211). Input outside the grammar accepted in
silence is the failure family this corpus exists to find. Pinned by
`reserved_accepts_ungrammatical_literals_and_discards_them`, which drives
`compile` directly so the corpus files themselves stay grammatical, and which
fails with instructions when the parser learns to reject them.

### Imports bind at package scope, and a repeated import is a hard error

`services.ridl` imports only `Ratio`, not `Speed` or `Temperature`, because
`appendix-a.ridl` already imports those and imports bind per package, not per
file (ADR-0002 §1: "files within a package are purely organisational").

Repeating an import is not merely redundant — it is a hard **error**: TYPL-006,
"conflicting import … alias one of the colliding imports". The message is
written for two *different* symbols competing for one local name, which is not
the situation, and the only way to satisfy the checker is to give one file a
different local name for the same type. `resolve.rs` counts local-name
collisions without comparing the imported package path, so it fires for two
identical imports in the same file as well as across files of one package.
A defect to fix, not a curiosity.
